    <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4>What is the difference between masking and neutralization?</h4>
            <p>Masking involves covering a bad smell with a stronger, more pleasant fragrance. Neutralization involves a chemical reaction that changes the malodour molecule into an odourless compound.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4>Why is particle size important in air fresheners?</h4>
            <p>Particle size determines how long the fragrance stays in the air. Ideal sizes (30-60 microns) ensure the mist lingers without falling too quickly to the floor or being inhaled too deeply.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4>Which propellant is best for air fresheners?</h4>
            <p>LPG and DME are common for high-performance sprays due to their constant pressure. Compressed gases like Nitrogen are used for more eco-friendly, water-based formulations.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4>Can I manufacture water-based aerosols in tinplate cans?</h4>
            <p>Yes, but it requires the addition of corrosion inhibitors in the formulation to prevent the water from reacting with the metal can over time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4>How do I calculate the profit margins for an aerosol business?</h4>
            <p>Profits depend on formulation cost, packaging (can/valve/cap), filling speed, and distribution. Technical efficiency in manufacturing is the key to maximizing these margins.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h4>What are the safety requirements for an aerosol factory?</h4>
            <p>Factories must have explosion-proof electrical systems, gas detection sensors, proper ventilation, and strictly adhere to fire safety standards for flammable propellant storage.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

